<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Danish Greenland</h1><p class="st-meta">1721 CE – 1953 CE · capital Godthåb</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Greenland as a Danish colony, from the missionary Hans Egede's arrival in 1721 to its integration into the Danish realm in 1953.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hoping to find the lost Norse colonists, the Norwegian priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hans-egede">Hans Egede</a> sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> in 1721 with the support of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway</a> and founded a mission and trading post, moving it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuuk">Godthåb</a> (Nuuk) in 1728. Danish trading stations then spread along the west coast.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>From 1776 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-greenland-trading-department">Royal Greenland Trade Department</a> held a monopoly on trade, keeping the island closed to outsiders. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a> population lived by seal hunting, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> bought skins, blubber and later cod. Lutheran and Moravian missions converted most Greenlanders. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> was ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1814, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> stayed Danish, and in 1933 the Permanent Court of International Justice rejected Norway's claim to East Greenland.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>During the German occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> protected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> and built bases there. In 1953 a new Danish constitution ended colonial status and made Greenland an integral part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark</a>. Home rule followed in 1979 and self-government in 2009.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1721 CE – 1953 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:93.95%;width:4.62%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1721 CE – 1953 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Godthåb</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hans Egede</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran Christianity, Inuit religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greenlandic, Danish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greenland">Greenland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Greenland colony, Grønland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1837&amp;focus=danish-greenland" aria-label="Danish Greenland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3971 -128 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Danish Greenland lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5274 535h0M5274 535h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4291 128h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4291 128h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Greenland. <a href="/atlas?year=1837&amp;focus=danish-greenland">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

